    {
      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "Spook [BBB+19] is one of the 32 candidates that has made it to the second round of the NIST Lightweight Cryptography Standardiza- tion process, and is particularly interesting since it proposes differential side channel resistance. In this paper, we present practical distinguish- ers of the full 6-step version of the underlying permutations of Spook, namely Shadow-512 and Shadow-384, solving challenges proposed by the designers on the permutation. We also propose practical forgeries with 4-step Shadow for the S1P mode of operation in the nonce misuse sce- nario, which is allowed by the CIML2 security game considered by the authors. All the results presented in this paper have been implemented.",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
